The code statements that lead to the bug are:
newsk = unix_create1(net, NULL, 0, sock->type);
err = prepare_peercred(&peercred);
if (err)
    goto out;
Reason:
`prepare_peercred()` is called after `unix_create1()` allocates `newsk`. If `prepare_peercred()` fails, the error path jumps to `out`, which does not free `newsk`, causing a memory leak.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
err = prepare_peercred(&peercred);
if (err)
    goto out;
newsk = unix_create1(net, NULL, 0, sock->type);
(and removal of the original `prepare_peercred` call after `unix_create1`)
Reason:
By moving `prepare_peercred()` before `unix_create1()`, any failure occurs before `newsk` is allocated, so the `out` label (which only cleans up `peercred`) is safe. This prevents the memory leak because `newsk` is never created when `prepare_peercred()` fails.

